  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMorning water vapor imagery shows the remnants of Isaias moving \nnorth over the southeast US and an upper low across the Pacific \nNorthwest. In between these features, mid-level ridging holds across \nthe Southern and Central Plains and will remain so through the \nweekend, keeping dry conditions and above-normal temperatures in \nplace. Breezy south-southwest winds gusting at 20-35 MPH are \nexpected this afternoon and again Sunday with highs topping out \naround 90 degrees. Slightly warmer 850mb temperatures build overhead \non Monday, leading to the warmest day of this stretch with morning \nlows near 70 degrees and highs in the low 90s.\n\nPredictability decreases Tuesday through the middle of next week as \nguidance differs substantially on when the closed low ejects across \nthe Plains and how strong this wave will be. Ahead of the closed \nlow, ensembles are in good agreement in a surface boundary sagging \ninto the area Tuesday, but how far south the front progresses is \nuncertain. Highs Tuesday will be dependent on the location of the \nfront; highs will likely be near 90 degrees again ahead of the \nboundary, but only around 70 degrees behind the front. Large-scale \nascent is weak, but frontal forcing could allow for showers and \nstorms to develop Tuesday afternoon and evening. A few storms could \nbe strong ahead of the boundary given shear around 30 kts and modest \ninstability. As the western closed low approaches, it ingests \nTropical Storm Rachel and Hurricane Simon, with energy from Simon \napproaching the forecast area Tuesday night into Wednesday and \nincreasing precipitation chances. Ensembles have been trending \nweaker and slower with the western trough. About 60% of GEFS, ENS, \nand GEPS members keep the trough west of the area through Thursday \nwhile the remaining 40% are more progressive and stronger with the \nwave. The environment could be favorable for some strong to severe \nstorms during this time, but details are far too tough to discern at \nthis range, especially given the uncertainty. Temperatures are \nfavored to trend back to near-normal values by the end of next week.",
